Z||000004||jelly2m8||12-06-2001||08:49 PM||||Should be easy enough to find a replacement decklid hinge. They are all interchangable through the years.<P> Just be careful working around that torsion rod, theres a sh!tload of tension on them!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||REDHOTT88||12-06-2001||08:53 PM|||| I believe ya! I could feel it pushing it down..<P>Not much of a hinge now that i look at it...<BR>No wonder it broke!<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rodney||12-06-2001||09:54 PM||||One big problem is the pin shearing. The mild steel pin and bore of the hinge rusts which eventually makes the pin fail. How many of you have ever taken the time to oil your rear deck lid hinge pins? I have never even done it. Should be done ocassionally. Someday I'll have replacement pins. I don't think they are too hard to rebuild. I have the straps but they are not on my web page yet. ||||reg||1
